281103 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "95e414d6-af68-52cc-a7c3-68806836a587" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281142 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186278782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281142 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186279043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281142 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186279157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281142 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186279361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281142 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280368 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281142 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281142 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281142 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186279361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281142 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b05d6e1c-d362-5f58-a922-c7bdfcb8ad48" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281198 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186279359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281198 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281198 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281198 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281198 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281198 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281198 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281198 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186280796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281198 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d0016591-9999-5fe2-ae9e-6b503dd172a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281287 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281287 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161657" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281287 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281287 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the volume of the solid $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,1\\le z\\le 3,\\,0\\le x\\le 2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281287 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2cb18357-7f8d-5c17-b356-ff0cf34ef3fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281287 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that we have a three-dimensional type II region.\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}[x]_{x=0}^{x=2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,{[{z}^{2}]}_{z=1}^{z=3}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y({3}^{2}-{1}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy \\\\  & =8[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{y=0}^{y=\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-8(0-1) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281288 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280948 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281288 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161659" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281288 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281288 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the volume of the solid $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}},\\,0\\le y\\le 2z\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281288 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "92ae0dd1-b6f7-54c8-bc54-cc5a6fb1fbb3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281288 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that we have a three-dimensional type III region.\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}{\\int }_{0}^{2z}\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}[y]_{y=0}^{y=2z}\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}2z\\,dz\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{[{z}^{2}]}_{z=-0}^{z=\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{(\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}})}^{2}-0\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}2-{x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={[2x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{x=-2}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(2(2)-\\frac{1}{3}(2)^{3})-(2(-2)-\\frac{1}{3}(-2)^{3}) \\\\  & =(4-\\frac{8}{3})-(-4+\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281292 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281292 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161677" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281292 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281292 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the volume of the solid region enclosed by the $xy$-plane, the surface $z=4-{x}^{2},$ and the planes $x=0,$ $x=2,$ $y=0,$ $y=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281292 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a372c14-9ab9-5f40-9e05-8b6bd0720902" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281292 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that our solid can be written as a type I region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-{x}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-{x}^{2}}\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-{x}^{2}}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}4-{x}^{2}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})[y]_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(4-{x}^{2})(2-0)\\,dx \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2}4-{x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\  & =2{[4x-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =2(4(2)-\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3}))-0 \\\\  & =2(\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281293 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280934 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281293 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161680" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281293 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281293 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the volume of the solid region enclosed by the planes $x=1-y$ and $x=y+1,$ and the planes $y=1,$ $y=2,$ $z=0,$ $z=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281293 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "58d3fcb5-6807-58ac-a1ca-bf0f74191add" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281293 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that our solid can be written as a type II region: \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,1-y\\le x\\le y+1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1-y}^{y+1}\\,dx\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[x]_{x=1-y}^{x=y+1}\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}y+1-(1-y)\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,dz\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y[z]_{z=0}^{z=1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}2y\\,dy \\\\  & =[{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281300 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186281142 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281300 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161696" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281300 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281300 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cf/cf19d77966b23604f18c4194b4166536ff2f12267cbc55a98a436bda9fc3c098.png)\n\nFind the volume of the solid region above.\n\n*Hint: All surfaces, except for the top one, are parallel to the corresponding coordinate planes.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281300 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cf85e5fc-e5a2-5308-98bc-5ce96212e0b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281300 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/a7/a7261baec0c907779f5b1f5ff669ca826d4b93c5dd1b9a389e0989fe34756a6e.png)\n\n \n\nNotice that our solid can be written as the type I region\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-x-2y\\},\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nis the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane, shown below.\n\n![](images/65/65c61ad7dc2964cd0de9a6bbdf259fcf3388e25da4eaabf155d58ff0c05c8d55.png)\n\nHence, the type I representation of $R$ is\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-x-2y\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of $R$ is given by the following triple integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-2y}dz\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[z]_{z=0}^{z=4-x-2y}\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4-x-2y)-0\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4-x-2y\\,dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[(4-x)y-{y}^{2}]_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}4-x-1\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3-x\\,dx \\\\  & ={[3x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =(6-2)-0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362409 #edb/ref 1064 "When a solid is described in words, we first identify its bounding surfaces and then decide how to describe the entire region with inequalities.\n\nA useful strategy is:\n\n- identify the variable that lies between a lower surface and an upper surface,\n\n- determine the projection of the solid onto a coordinate plane,\n\n- write bounds for the projected region,\n\n- use those bounds to set up a triple integral with integrand equal to ![](images/95/95836ae470c61ab15bfb67d6f0c92d2ad8d03d400b04473e81e58de3ee44bf32.svg).\n\nIn many volume problems, the solid lies between a bottom surface\n![](images/8e/8eeea9a9caa745d87342df718b249603d257e35820bea2df1f1d647d61877c0c.svg)\nand a top surface\n![](images/21/211a455fb9c563450b5b5225f0d3615467c89870e467920ad73beacb802375b8.svg)\n\nIn that case, we describe the solid by\n![](images/eb/eb36f1bb66ca8c06c73de972395684c368cc3cdba4d05b9300dee9f852c3174b.svg)\nwhere ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is the projection of the solid onto the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane.\n\nSo, to find the volume, we use\n![](images/c6/c6fd71393350dac5cd3d1067227863cab1e92534812ea083231408ce660143bf.svg)\n\nSo, the verbal description tells us two things:\n\n- which surface is below and which is above,\n\n- which points ![](images/e7/e76592a69e19fffc93e0e39005deba219d59736a203a18c95ebbe2496bd772a7.svg) belong to the base region ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg).\n\nBefore integrating, it is always a good idea to check that the upper surface is actually above the lower surface on the entire projected region." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362409 #edb/ref 1065 15980 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362409 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8f80f606-7a23-56be-8c78-48b9eeaa0fd7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362409 #edb/ref 1067 "Interpreting a Verbal Description of a Solid" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362410 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the volume of the solid region enclosed by the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane, the plane ![](images/af/af57fba1cdb37044c8a4f922ba0ce95fcae5347960e050ac391af840198aca28.svg) and the planes ![](images/89/89c97ad93c125fc2b6f61d44fe9a90845fd289329d79d506c857f970d7586540.svg) ![](images/48/480183a1321e5b4fdff9500a551818f6fb0ab2713c0fdd8fa9df88a640394af9.svg) ![](images/8d/8de04eef03bf4f5fd455bcd39f1d747bc5e38650a1f8c6dd5249166cf5fc022d.svg) and ![](images/ca/ca7dadeea1df352335554e1c48b6398167035636994354744737e4c75a1b0134.svg)\n\nNotice that our solid can be written as a type I region.\n\nFirst, the phrase **enclosed by the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane** tells us that the bottom of the solid is\n![](images/ef/efcfb7a56c85727ded8a9d5c9bd75fd79ac0bc20ba202c2da6aaca3c4e3ba503.svg)\n\nThe plane\n![](images/21/211cfe0529803c8450173dbcaa60c66f276389b5944047bc390738703f657309.svg)\nis the top of the solid.\n\nAlso, the planes\n![](images/a3/a3fe77f111727e68d20276851f67f7fc662eaea5df49eaf96695a9234f6304bf.svg)\ndescribe the projection of the solid onto the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane. So the base region is the rectangle\n![](images/59/5925cb0d118a2a3457f4683c7b38bfa59bc5e8bbc106af32f0364a0207f9af25.svg)\n\nTherefore, for each point ![](images/e7/e76592a69e19fffc93e0e39005deba219d59736a203a18c95ebbe2496bd772a7.svg) in this rectangle, the variable ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg) runs from the bottom surface to the top surface:\n![](images/b1/b1af88023edd0b854bb6b598521693ebf4e941d8a5567e6801801f1deeacb902.svg)\n\nSo the volume is\n![](images/bd/bd9acbbd0fb9472aafdc28fe38d7cf6a103c123ff999116cefca75f8dbf624a8.svg)\n\nNow we evaluate:\n\n![](images/fa/fa67106261e7d847a0be115b74ca3ebaa5d5aff7ceca30b0b94b950fb1b741cd.svg)\n\nTherefore, the volume of the solid is ![](images/85/85ab4d14e4ae710116ac84de4b03b3cf35723ab969b7abfba97889c497974147.svg)\n\nIn problems like this, the planes involving only ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg) and ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg) usually describe the base region in the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane, while the ![](images/39/39303ee53d6cd0a654e183557bb493fd0ba5b887f96f5d7d871a9be772ce1808.svg)-plane and the surface or plane involving ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg) describe the lower and upper bounds for ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg)." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362410 #edb/ref 1065 15981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362410 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ca3b6881-db7f-536a-b48b-77adde0a1b19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362410 #edb/ref 1067 "Setting Up Volume from a Verbal Description" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362411 #edb/ref 1064 "Alternatively, we can conceptualize the volume of a